      "Athena puts beauty on Penelope": "Is made taller, whiter, and more beautiful in her sleep by Athena, and goes down to the hall against her own inclination to show herself to the suitors — and extracts a robe, a necklace, earrings, and a chain from them in the process. Odysseus is delighted, because she is charming gifts out of men she means to see dead.",
      "Penelope and the stranger": "Sits opposite the beggar by the fire and questions him, and gets a Cretan lie so well made that Homer says she wept as she listened to it, and that his words were like snow melting on the mountains.",
      "The brooch and the tunic": "Tests the stranger's story by asking what Odysseus was wearing, and is given the golden brooch with the hound throttling a fawn and the tunic like dry onion-skin — an exact description of things she gave him herself, which convinces her of the story without convincing her of the man.",
      "The geese, and the gates of horn and ivory": "Tells her dream of twenty geese killed by an eagle who declares himself her husband, sets out the gates of horn and ivory, and then announces the contest of the bow for the next day — the decision the whole endgame runs on.",
      "Penelope's prayer for death": "Wakes weeping and prays to Artemis to shoot her now, or to have a storm carry her off, rather than gladden a lesser man — and her voice reaches Odysseus lying awake in the porch below.",
      "Penelope goes to the storeroom": "Takes the bronze key with the ivory handle, opens the storeroom whose doors bellow like a bull, lifts her husband's bow down from its peg, and sits with it across her knees and cries before carrying it out to the men who will fail with it.",
      "Penelope sent upstairs": "Defends the beggar's right to try the bow, is told by her son that the bow is men's business and his most of all, and goes up astonished at him — where Athena puts her to sleep through the entire killing.",
      "Penelope refuses to believe": "Comes down and sits opposite her husband in silence across the fire, refusing every proof that anyone else could have known, on the stated grounds that many men make their living from plausible stories.",
      "The secret of the olive-tree bed": "Sets the poem's last and best trap by telling Eurycleia to move the bed out of the chamber, and gets the one reaction only her husband could produce. Then the simile of land seen by drowning swimmers is given to her, not to him.",
